  "summary": "The practice of repurposing existing buildings for new functions while preserving their historical character and architectural features.",
  "description": "Characterized by visible juxtapositions of old and new elements, exposed original materials like brick and timber, and contemporary insertions within historical envelopes. It emerged from urban preservation movements and sustainability concerns, transforming obsolete industrial or historical buildings into vibrant new spaces with layered temporal narratives.",
  "culturalContext": "The aesthetic of keeping building history visible while converting use — Tate Modern from Bankside Power Station, the High Line from freight rail, Neues Museum from wartime ruin. Rising with deindustrialization in the 1970s-90s, it replaced demolition culture with a conservation ethic: old structure celebrated as narrative texture, new interventions (Rhode's one-legged steel stair,Chipperfield's repaired-not-restored masonry) legible against it. The style anchors contemporary cultural architecture worldwide.",
